  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSimilar programming to last night for the weather pattern. A surface \nhigh over northern IL has advanced a little further south and a bit \nfurther west. Likewise, the H500 high over NM continues to send \nridge running shortwaves around the system. Once again, these \nshortwaves trigger showers and thunderstorms across the Sand Hills \nand the foothills of the Rockies. Additional convection is \nanticipated across central and eastern KS through the overnight. \nLike Wednesday morning, the surface high is expected to limit the \nability for showers and storms to migrate eastward. Expectation are \nfor storms to approach far eastern KS before weakening as they \napproach the MO/KS state line. A few sprinkles and showers are \nexpected to make it into the I-29/I-49 corridors through the early \nmorning.\n\nLee cyclogenesis across western KS catalyzed by a notable upper \nlevel shortwave will be the primary influence of our weather through \nthe day. Broad scale southerly flow once again advects warm air and \nmoisture into the region. CAMs show a combination of turbulent \nsurface convergence (largely due to some variability in low level \nwinds), isentropic ascent, and some mesoscale upper level lift from \nsmaller H500 perturbations. This keeps isolated chances for showers \nand storms around through much of the day for places west of the I-\n35/I-49 corridors. Most likely we will see something similar to \nyesterday where isolated to scattered light showers move through the \narea through the daytime hours.\n\nThrough Thursday afternoon, another lee cyclone develops across NW \nNebraska in the more progressive flow across the Upper Midwest. A \ncold front extends from this system and the frontogenetic energy \nhelps enhance the smaller wave anticipated over eastern KS. Low \nlevel southerly flow accelerates advecting more warm air and \nmoisture into the area ahead of the systems arrival. The complex of \nshowers and thunderstorms is expected to work its way into the \nregion after midnight. Guidance continues to maintain fairly \nprogressive movement of the thunderstorm complex with it exiting the \nregion just past 8am. Elevated dew points and PWATs around 2 inches \ncontinue to favor an environment for torrential downpours; however, \nthe progressive movement of the storms does limit several \nhydrological concerns. The trade off is the faster storm motions \nbring in the potential for damaging winds. The convective \nenvironment shows just enough shear and instability to be a bit \nconcerned for an isolated strong to severe storm with the most \nlikely hazard being damaging wind gusts.\n\nUnfortunately, the passing front does not do too much to curtail \nheat for the day Friday. Cyclogenesis persists across the I-80 \ncorridor and turns winds westerly advecting hot air from the \nfoothills of the Rockies into the area. Temperatures along and south \nof I-70 rise into the 90s. Those north of the MO River will see a \nmore NW flow of the winds which lowers highs into the mid to upper \n80s across far northern MO. Additional showers and storms are \npossible Friday afternoon as a synoptic scale front follows behind \nthe overnight system. Warm air advection from the west looks to \ndestabilize the region and lead to the development of additional \nshowers and thunderstorms ahead of the front. Depending on how much \nthe atmosphere is able to recharge, some robust convection may \ndevelop. The likely area for these potentially stronger storms is \nacross SW and Central MO. It is plausible for some strong to severe \nstorms with heavy rain, damaging winds, and hail being the likely \nhazards. Behind the synoptic front, temperatures fall substantially \nwith highs Saturday expected to be in the low to mid 80s. \n\nThe extended forecast continues to show eventual breakdown of the \nlarge H500 ridge over the SW CONUS. This opens up the pattern to \npotentially become a little more active next week as the more active \nzonal flow which has resided across the northern CONUS for the past \nseveral days is able to progress further into the interior CONUS. \nLikewise, southerly flow reopens and sends temperatures back upward \ninto the 90s next week.",
